<h3>Introduction</h3>
<p>
  This app provides a long-term history of the Australian stock market from 1883 to today, with a matching
  United States series for comparison (switch markets at the top of the page). For each market it features annual returns for:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>The stock price index</li>
  <li>The stock price index with dividends reinvested</li>
  <li>10-year government bonds</li>
  <li>The Consumer Price Index (CPI)</li>
</ul>
<p>
  These annual returns are used to construct both nominal and inflation-adjusted long-term indices, as well as
  <a href="https://www.investopedia.com/terms/r/rollingreturns.asp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">rolling returns</a>.
</p>

<hr>

<h3>Australian data</h3>

<h4>Primary Sources</h4>
<p>
  The core annual return data (1883–2010) are sourced from
  <a href="https://research.bond.edu.au/en/publications/the-historical-equity-risk-premium-in-australia-post-gfc-and-128-" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
    Brailsford et al. (2012)
  </a>,
  which extends earlier work by
  <a href="https://research.bond.edu.au/en/publications/re-examination-of-the-historical-equity-risk-premium-in-australia" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
    Brailsford et al. (2008)
  </a>.
</p>

<h5>Brailsford et al. (2008)</h5>
<p>
  Brailsford et al. (2008) constructed a historical Australian stock price series from 1883–2005 by combining three major indices:
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Commercial and Industrial Index (1882–1936)</li>
  <li>Sydney All Ordinary Shares Price Index (1936–1979)</li>
  <li>ASX All Ordinaries Price Index (1980–2005)</li>
</ol>
<p>
  For data up to December 1957, they relied on the work of
  <a href="https://searchworks.stanford.edu/view/1408035" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Don Lamberton</a>,
  who retrospectively compiled:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>The Commercial Industrial Price Index (Jan 1875–Jun 1936)</li>
  <li>The All Ordinary Shares Price Index (Jul 1936–Dec 1957)</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Brailsford et al. (2008) used December values to form their annual series and incorporated historical dividend yields to create an accumulation index.
</p>

<h5>Brailsford et al. (2012)</h5>
<p>
  <a href="https://research.bond.edu.au/en/publications/the-historical-equity-risk-premium-in-australia-post-gfc-and-128-" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
    Brailsford et al. (2012)
  </a>
  extended the series to 2010 by:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Using the average December values of the All Ordinaries Accumulation and Price Indices (2006–2010)</li>
  <li>Recording 10-year bond yields at each December’s end</li>
  <li>Incorporating December CPI values from the Australian Bureau of Statistics</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h4>Data Extension (2011–Present)</h4>
<p>
  For years 2011 to today, the same methodology was applied:
</p>
<ol>
  <li>
    <strong>Stock Indices:</strong>
    December averages of the
    <a href="http://au.spindices.com/indices/equity/all-ordinaries" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      S&amp;P Dow Jones Indices All Ordinaries (Price Return and Total Return)
    </a>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>10-Year Bonds:</strong>
    December-end yields from the
    <a href="https://www.rba.gov.au/statistics/tables/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      RBA’s historical data
    </a>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Inflation (CPI):</strong>
    December values from the
    <a href="https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/economy/price-indexes-and-inflation/consumer-price-index-australia/latest-release" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      ABS CPI series
    </a>
  </li>
</ol>
<p>
  Since 2025 the ABS publishes a monthly CPI, so inflation from 2025 is December to December
  rather than December quarter to December quarter. The All Ordinaries are exported by hand each year from S&amp;P Dow Jones Indices
  and processed with <code>code/update.R</code>, which first checks that it reproduces the previously published years.
</p>

<hr>

<h3>United States data</h3>
<p>
  The US series cover the same years and follow the same layout as the Australian ones. They combine two public datasets:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    Robert J. Shiller,
    <a href="https://shillerdata.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Online Data</a>
    (<em>ie_data.xls</em>): monthly S&amp;P Composite prices, dividends, CPI and long-term government bond yields from 1871,
    as used in his book <em>Irrational Exuberance</em>.
  </li>
  <li>
    Aswath Damodaran,
    <a href="https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/New_Home_Page/datafile/histret.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      Historical Returns on Stocks, Bonds and Bills</a>,
    NYU Stern School of Business: year-end S&amp;P 500 levels and returns from 1928.
  </li>
  <li>
    Kenneth R. French,
    <a href="https://mba.tuck.dartmouth.edu/pages/faculty/ken.french/data_library.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Data Library</a>
    (Fama/French 3 Factors, daily), Tuck School of Business, Dartmouth: daily returns of the whole US share market with dividends
    (the CRSP value-weighted index) from July 1926. Used for the worst falls at daily closes.
  </li>
  <li>
    Yardeni Research,
    <a href="https://old.yardeni.com/wp-content/uploads/BullBearTables.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stock Market Historical Tables: Bull &amp; Bear Markets</a>
    (January 2024), from Standard &amp; Poor's data: the table of S&amp;P 500 bear markets at daily closes.
  </li>
</ul>
<ol>
  <li>
    <strong>Stock indices, 1883–1927:</strong> Shiller's data, December to December. Shiller's prices are monthly averages of
    daily closes, and the total return adds one twelfth of the annual dividend each month, as in Shiller's own total return series.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Stock indices, 1928 on:</strong> Damodaran's year-end S&amp;P 500 level (price) and S&amp;P 500 return with dividends.
    These match commonly quoted calendar-year returns (for example −37% in 2008), which December averages do not.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>10-year bonds:</strong> Shiller's December long-term government bond yield (the 10-year Treasury yield in recent decades),
    used as that year's return, the same treatment as the Australian bonds. Bond price changes are not included.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Inflation (CPI):</strong> December-to-December change in Shiller's CPI series (the US Consumer Price Index from 1913,
    earlier estimates before that).
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Falls:</strong> the US falls chart uses Shiller's monthly data from December 1882, so falls that recover
    within a year (1987, 2020) show. Each month is the average of that month's daily closes, which hides the single worst days:
    in 2020 US shares fell about 19% month to month against 34% at daily closes. So the worst fall on the shares + dividends card uses
    daily closes from July 1926 (Kenneth R. French's daily US market returns, with dividends, joined to the monthly series at June 1926);
    after inflation, each day is deflated by that month's CPI. On that basis 1929–32 was a fall of 84%, against 82% from monthly averages.
    The price-only figures stay monthly, as no free daily price series goes back that far. In the monthly series, total return adds one
    twelfth of the annual dividend each month, and bonds earn the previous month's 10-year yield for the month. Australian falls are
    year-end only, as no long monthly Australian history is available.
  </li>
</ol>
<p>
  <strong>Comparing the two markets:</strong> the Australian share series use December averages from 2006 and the US series use
  year-end values from 1928, so individual years can differ by a few points for that reason alone. Long-run growth rates are unaffected.
  Each market is shown in its own currency.
</p>

<hr>

<h3 id="dividend-method">Dividend calculator: method</h3>
<p>
  The calculator follows an amount put into shares at the end of the first selected year, valued at the end of each later year
  in two ways. The data hold two yearly returns for each market: the <strong>total return</strong>, which includes dividends,
  and the <strong>price return</strong>, which leaves them out. The calculator builds everything from these two.
</p>
<ol>
  <li>
    <strong>Dividends reinvested</strong> follows the total return: amount × (1 + total return) for each year. Every dividend buys
    more shares, which then rise and pay dividends of their own. This is the same series as on the Growth tab.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Dividends taken as cash</strong> keeps the same shares and adds up the dividends they paid:
    <ul>
      <li>the shares' value follows the price return: amount × (1 + price return) for each year;</li>
      <li>each year's dividend is the gap between the two returns, applied to the shares' value at the start of that year:
        <em>dividend = value at start of year × (total return − price return)</em>;</li>
      <li>the dividends are added up as cash that earns no interest.</li>
    </ul>
    The line is the shares' value plus all dividends paid so far.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Extra from reinvesting</strong> is the difference between the two. It is what the reinvested dividends earned on
    top of themselves: the compounding.
  </li>
</ol>
<h5>Worked example: $1,000 in US shares at the end of 2023</h5>
<table class="method-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Shares worth</th><th>Dividend that year</th><th>Dividends so far</th><th>Taken as cash</th><th>Reinvested</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>2023</td><td>$1,000</td><td>–</td><td>$0</td><td>$1,000</td><td>$1,000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2024</td><td>$1,233</td><td>$1,000 × (24.9% − 23.3%) = $15.69</td><td>$15.69</td><td>$1,249</td><td>$1,249</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2025</td><td>$1,435</td><td>$1,233 × 1.34% = $16.47</td><td>$32.16</td><td>$1,467</td><td>$1,470</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>
  After one year the two are equal. From the second year the reinvested dividends earn returns of their own, so the gap
  opens. It is small over two years, and over decades it becomes most of the final value.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Adjusted for inflation:</strong> the shares' value and each year's dividend are converted to first-year dollars
  using prices at the end of that year, so every figure shows buying power.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Limits:</strong>
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Dividends are estimated from the gap between the two returns, not taken from records of actual payments.</li>
  <li>The Australian share returns from 2006 are based on December averages, so single years' dividends are approximate.
    Totals over many years are reliable.</li>
  <li>Dividends taken as cash are assumed to earn nothing. Cash kept in a bank account would have earned some interest,
    which would narrow the gap somewhat.</li>
  <li>Tax, fees and franking credits are not included.</li>
</ul>

